    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d659">

                <pb facs="0923" n="797" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg797"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.00/93: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacMurray</hi>)<anchor n="4" xml:id="d659-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-07-23T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 23, 1925—3
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>165. Department’s 125, July 1, Noon, and text of Japanese proposal communicated
                    to you by Tokyo July 22.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d659-f2"/></p>
                <p>Department has now received British Embassy’s reply to its note, text of which
                    was communicated to you in its No. 148 of July 14, 5 p.m.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d659-f3"/></p>
                <p>[Here follows the substance of the three notes from the British Chargé, dated
                    July 20, printed on pages <ref target="#pg788">788</ref>–<ref target="#pg791"
                        >791</ref>; also the text of the Department’s reply, dated July 23, printed
                        <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>, which contains an alternate draft reply
                    to the Chinese note of June 24.]</p>
                <p>You will use above quoted text as basis for discussing with your colleagues the
                    texts of replies which will be made to Chinese Government’s note and evidence of
                    the attitude that this Government desires to take. Department does not insist on
                    wording as given but desires opportunity to approve any suggested changes.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Tokyo as Department’s No. 92 omitting last paragraph, referring to
                    Tokyo’s No. 131, July 21, 5 p.m. to Department.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d659-f4"/>
                    Instruct Tokyo to communicate text of Department’s reply to British Embassy to
                    Japanese Foreign Office for its information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d659-f1" n="4" type="source" xml:id="d659-f1-content">
                <p>See last paragraph for instructions to repeat to Tokyo as Department’s No.
                    92.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d659-f2" n="5" xml:id="d659-f2-content">
                <p>The Japanese proposal referred to is the same as the Japanese draft reply to the
                    Chinese note of June 24, handed to the Secretary by the Japanese Ambassador on
                    July 21, <ref target="#pg792">p. 792</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d659-f3" n="6" xml:id="d659-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 148 not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d659-f4" n="7" xml:id="d659-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
